Z||000007||thomas_l||05-28-2001||12:32 PM||||Those cracks won't go away with out a repaint. All wet sanding is going to do is take out surface damage. My paint will still look like sh*t on close inspection, but the surface will be slick and shiny. Besides, if you sand thru the clear and see color in the water then it's too late. At that point stopping will just prevent more damage. I have a few spots like that and it's just a trade off in my case - a few ugly spots for an overall big improvement in the appearance. Later this summer I will probably just paint the whole thing.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||05-28-2001||06:39 PM||||I wet sand clear urathane with 1000 then rub it out with 3M superduty compound then finish with fine cut and swirl remover on lighter colors. darker colors I go back over it with 1200 or 1500 before buffing. A trick to keep from 'burning' the edges is do one panel at a time, use masking tape on the edges of adjoining panels as you work your way around. Also on ridges always let the buffer spin off of the edge not onto it. Hand rub along edges of rubber like around the windshield or youll mess up the rubber piece permanently. after youre done with compound wash it all off immediately with lots of water, It dries like concrete. even a brush is good to get it out of seams.<P>only sand it when its white, if you start to get color wet sanding you went too far. You have to reclear it. You can make basecoat shine but it goes away real quick.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 05-28-2001).]||||reg||1
